You can achieve this by creating a new location block that catches requests for specific posts and redirects them to the www subdomain.

Here's an updated version of your configuration that adds a new location block to redirect posts:
```nginx
server {
  listen 443 ssl http2;
  listen [::]:443 ssl http2;
  server_name askfundu.com www.askfundu.com;
  root /usr/share/nginx/html;

  # SSL
  ssl_certificate /etc/nginx/server.crt;
  ssl_certificate_key /etc/nginx/private.key;

  # logging
  access_log /var/log/nginx/access.log combined buffer=512k flush=1m;
  error_log /var/log/nginx/error.log warn;

  # index.html fallback
  location / {
    root /usr/share/nginx/html;
    index index.html index.htm;
    try_files $uri $uri/ /index.html;
  }

  error_page 500 502 503 504 /50x.html;

  location = /50x.html {
    root /usr/share/nginx/html;
  }

  # Redirect posts to www subdomain
  location ~ ^/(.*)$ {
    return 301 https://www.askfundu.com/$1;
  }
}
```
In this updated configuration, we added a new location block that matches any URL (${location ~ ^/(.*)$ }) and redirects it to the www subdomain using the `return 301` directive. The `$1` in the redirect URL refers to the captured group (the post URL).

For example, if a user requests `https://askfundu.com/post/abc`, the server will redirect them to `https://www.askfundu.com/post/abc`.

Note that we also removed the non-www subdomain redirect server block, as the new location block will handle the redirection for you.

